CONTEXT — WHY THIS EXISTS

The Phase 1 work spanned three weeks inside a single Cursor chat that
grew to ~10,000 lines. That chat held everything — investigations, runs,
hypotheses, postmortems, tech-lead messages — and became impossible to
navigate or onboard from. The KANBAN folder was created to dissect that
chat into a fractal triad structure: every investigation and every run
has DESCRIPTION (the question / the configuration), OBSERVATIONS (the
evidence and belief evolution), and NEXT_STEPS (what to do next or what
this spawned). The triad is recursive and intentionally minimal — see
KANBAN/PROTOCOL.md for the rules.

The migration was done in a single pass and is structurally sound but
under-filled. Most run folders capture the headline result but miss the
intelligence layer: the reasoning that justified each next run, the
research the team did mid-investigation, the rejected branches, the
numeric context that makes the result interpretable.

HOW TO READ THE CURSOR CHAT EXPORT

The exported chat is a long flat text file alternating between user and
assistant turns. Each turn is typically marked by a header like
`## User` or `## Assistant`. There are no timestamps inside the turns
themselves; chronology comes from position in the file.

Practical tips for actually READING it rather than scanning:

- Search by anchor strings, not by topic. The reliable anchors are:
  W&B display names (e.g. `cerulean-snow-13`), W&B run IDs (e.g.
  `1chv2608`, `jhodg49x`), commit hashes (e.g. `5e78caa`, `62b94dd`,
  `ffc33ed`, `562de1b`), exact command strings (e.g.
  `--lambda-var 0.5`, `--horizon-k 12`), and distinctive metric
  values (e.g. `c_effective_rank` jumping from a specific value).
  These anchors are unambiguous; topic words like "collapse" or
  "slot" return too many hits to be useful.

- Triangulate every run from at least three places: the launch
  command (often inside a tmux block or a bash code fence), the
  step=N metric printouts that follow, and the post-run interpretive
  discussion between user and assistant. A run that appears only as
  a single mention is probably a smoke test, not a real training run.

- The exact command run on the pod often appears in TWO forms in the
  same vicinity: a proposed command (in the assistant's draft) and
  the user's pasted terminal output showing what was actually run.
  The pasted terminal output is the ground truth. If they disagree
  — and they sometimes do, because the user edits flags before
  launching — trust the terminal output.

- Reasoning lives in the assistant turns AROUND the user's pasted
  metric blocks. Read the assistant turn immediately before the
  pasted metrics (the proposed hypothesis / launch reasoning) AND
  the assistant turn immediately after (the interpretation). Both
  matter. The "why we chose this run" is usually in the BEFORE turn;
  the "what we learned" is in the AFTER turn.

- Rejected alternatives are often listed inline in assistant turns,
  formatted as tables or "Option A / Option B / Option C" blocks
  with a recommendation. The user's next turn usually picks one.
  Capture the rejected options when reconstructing why a run was
  the next logical step.

- External research surfaces in two ways: (a) the assistant
  explicitly citing a paper or technique (VICReg, SIGReg, Slot
  Attention, Perceiver, adaLN-Zero, I-JEPA, V-JEPA, DiT) usually
  when proposing a fix, and (b) the user pasting a quote from an
  "external source" or "Claude Fabel" — these are external review
  comments. The team's response to those reviews is part of the
  intellectual record.

- Tech-lead exchanges appear when the user says something like
  "send a message to my tech lead" or pastes a tech-lead reply.
  These are not the research record themselves, but the user's
  reaction and the assistant's follow-up often capture key
  decisions (e.g. the decision to try SIGReg vs not, the decision
  to halve coarse flow LR). Include the decision; skip the
  message drafting.

- The chat is non-linear in places. The user sometimes pastes log
  output from a run mid-discussion of a DIFFERENT topic, and
  sometimes asks meta-questions ("can you explain X simply") that
  interrupt a flow. Don't assume every paste belongs to the run
  most recently discussed — re-anchor on W&B names / IDs /
  commands.

- Some turns are scaffolding: the user asking how tmux works, the
  assistant explaining bf16, etc. These are educational, not
  research-record material. Skip them unless they reveal a
  realization that affected a run (e.g. the discussion about
  centered-vs-raw cosine that exposed the slot-loss-metric
  mismatch — that one matters).

- When the chat references files inside the codebase
  (`config.py`, `losses.py`, `models.py`, `train.py`,
  `diagnostics.py`, the `AGENT_FILES/` tree, or the `logs/` tree),
  cross-check by opening those files. Code-level realizations
  often live half in chat and half in commit messages or file
  comments.


READING STRATEGY FOR A 10,000-LINE CHAT

Do not attempt a single linear read. The chat is too long for reliable end-to-end recall in one pass. Use this multi-pass strategy instead:
Pass 1 — Anchor-first scan (do this before writing anything)

Extract all anchors from the full file in one sweep. Anchors are: W&B run names (e.g. cerulean-snow-13), run IDs (e.g. 1chv2608, jhodg49x), git commit hashes, exact CLI commands with their flags, and distinctive metric values. Write these down as a working index before reading any section in depth. These anchors are your navigation system for the rest of the task.
Pass 2 — Per-investigation deep read

For each investigation, use your anchor index to locate every relevant section. Read each located block as a triple: the assistant turn immediately BEFORE the pasted metrics (hypothesis / launch reasoning), the pasted metric block itself (the evidence), and the assistant turn immediately AFTER (the interpretation). All three together constitute one "finding." Missing any third of this triple means missing the intelligence layer.
Pass 3 — Cross-run synthesis

After Pass 2 for all investigations, do a final sweep for any turns where the user or assistant explicitly compares two runs, rejects a hypothesis, or describes a pivot. These pivots are often the most important content for NEXT_STEPS.md and investigation OBSERVATIONS.md.
If the file is truncated or unreadable in one context window:

Read it in overlapping chunks of ~2,000 lines. At the start of each chunk, re-read the last 200 lines of the previous chunk to maintain continuity. Do not write any KANBAN file until you have completed all chunks for that investigation.


