The new protocols to add:

1. CODE HYGIENE
   The agent keeps the codebase clean. When writing or modifying
   training code, regularizers, diagnostics, or any production path:
   - Prefer flags over hard-coded constants.
   - Default flags to current behavior so the baseline is one
     switch away.
   - Do not leave dead code behind; remove abandoned regularizers
     and unused branches when an investigation rejects them.
   - When in doubt, write less code.

2. INTERNAL CROSS-REFERENCE
   Before introducing a new mechanism, the agent reads the existing
   relevant investigation(s) and code to understand why the current
   approach was chosen. The KANBAN exists to prevent re-litigating
   settled questions. If a hypothesis was rejected and recorded in
   an investigation's OBSERVATIONS.md, the agent does not silently
   resurrect it without new evidence.

3. EXTERNAL RESEARCH
   When facing a novel problem not addressed by the existing
   investigations or codebase, the agent is encouraged to research
   external techniques (papers, established architectures, OSS
   implementations). A technique is worth proposing if either:
   (a) it has been demonstrated effective on the specific problem
   we're facing, or (b) it enhances the architecture while
   simultaneously addressing the open issue. The agent reports
   the source, the evidence, and the risk before proposing
   implementation.

4. EMPIRICAL GROUNDING VIA W&B
   The agent uses the W&B MCP server whenever a hypothesis or a
   fact-check would benefit from real run data. Specifically:
   - Before proposing a configuration change, check whether a
     similar configuration has been tried, and what happened.
   - When brainstorming next steps, pull recent run trajectories
     rather than relying on what the chat or files claim.
   - When debugging an unexpected metric, verify the trajectory
     in W&B before drawing conclusions.
   The agent does not invent metric values from memory; it pulls
   them.

